==Qualification==
{{Main|1962 FIFA World Cup qualification}}
[[Image:1962 world cup qualification.png|thumb|305px|{{legend|#000cff|Countries qualified for World Cup}} {{legend|#ffb400|Countries that failed to qualify}} {{legend|black|Countries that did not enter World Cup}} {{legend|#ababab|Country not a FIFA member}}]]
57 teams entered the 1962 World Cup (due to rejected entries and withdrawals, 52 teams eventually participated in the qualifying stages). Chile as host nation and Brazil as reigning World Cup champions were granted automatic qualification, with the remaining 14 finals places divided among the continental confederations.

Eight places were contested by [[UEFA]] teams (Europe) and three by [[CONMEBOL]] teams (South America). [[Confederation of African Football|CAF]] teams (Africa), [[Asian Football Confederation|AFC]] teams (Asia), [[North American Football Confederation|NAFC]] teams (North America), and [[Confederación Centroamericana y del Caribe de Fútbol|CCCF]] teams (Central America and Caribbean) contested three play-offs slots. The three winners would then face a European or South American team for entry into the World Cup. The 1962 tournament was the last one for which only nations from Europe or the Americas qualified.

Two teams qualified for the first time ever: [[Colombia national football team|Colombia]] and [[Bulgaria national football team|Bulgaria]].

[[Austria national football team|Austria]] withdrew during the qualification tournament due to financial problems.

[[Canada men's national soccer team|Canada]] withdrew during the qualification tournament due to scheduling constraints.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/nanaimo-daily-news-canada-pulls-out-of-1/20900536/|title=World Soccer Cup Schedule Too Tough|date=31 May 1960|access-date=21 June 2026|website=Nanaimo Daily News (archived on Newspapers.com)|archive-date=19 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250619042055/https://www.newspapers.com/article/nanaimo-daily-news-canada-pulls-out-of-1/20900536/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Italy, Switzerland and Uruguay all qualified for the first time since [[1954 FIFA World Cup|1954]], and Spain for the first time since [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]].

